------Runbook-style notes-------- 
How would someone else deploy and operate your environment?

        1) create a private/public key with ssh-keygen
	2) create s3 backend
	3) deploy IaC for the challenge.
	
	#note Complete one step at a time.
1)
#########   Create private/public key used to login to Bastion instance ######
To make sure you get acccess to Bastion:
(linux based) OS make it easier,  make sure you have created a public and private key.
ssh-keygen and enter for defaults most likely  would create in ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

if not default please update file with the right public key file name
modules/workloads/main.tf:  public_key = file("~/.ssh/<filename>.pub"
#######################################################################################
2)
######  create s3 backend    ########
clone repo
https://github.com/henmo6971/cf.git
cd cf, cd s3
run: terraform init, terraform plan; then terraform apply
########################################################
3)
###### Create the infra ###############
cd ../, on cf directory
run: terraform init ; terraform plan; terraform apply

Terraform plan/apply will prompt you for the IP to allow access to bastion.
you can use 0.0.0.0/0 or your IP x.x.x.x/32
######################################################

How would you restore data if the S3 bucket were deleted? 
	you have a couple of options here.
	- if S3 replication, or bacvkup  then you are safe :)
	- if there is a copy of the latest state file.
	- WORST CASE, rebuild the terraform state by importing resources

---------------- END of RUN BOOK NOTES ------------


once terraform creates all resources, lets navigate it.


#######################################
Navigating through the new environment.
ssh ec2-user@<Public IP BASTION ec2>

**Some extra to check httpd config**
You could also access via ssh to your ec2 instances in the ASG, not recommended, but i did for troubleshooting purposes.
cp from your terminal ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 to the bastion and name <file>.key and set chmod 400 to the new file.

then to ssh to private instances:
ssh -i <file>.key ec2-user@IP

